From: apklayer
Subject: [PATCH] libhidl: the host-forwarding stand-in sends the rest to the instance's own hwservicemanager

apklayer patch (docs/PATCHES.md, docs/HIDL.md, docs/HALIUM.md). An instance with host HALs (Halium: camera/media/graphics = device) keeps
libhidl's NoHwServiceManager stand-in (libhwbinder takes the real manager only when /system/bin/hwservicemanager exists, and then
the stand-in's forwarding of the host's names is gone). Next to it the stack now runs libapklayer_hwservicemanager and writes
/data/apklayer/hwservicemanager.local; with that marker every call of the stand-in that is not for a host HAL (get, add, addWithChain, getTransport,
list, listByInterface, listManifestByInterface, (un)registerForNotifications, client callbacks, tryUnregister) goes to the instance's context
manager (ProcessState::self(), not the host one), and the stand-in's old answers apply when it does not answer. Rebuild: `m libhidlbase`.
Against system/libhidl 061055fc4dad (REVISION), after 0001-0003. Apply: patches/apply aosp <LineageOS tree>.
---
--- a/transport/ServiceManagement.cpp
+++ b/transport/ServiceManagement.cpp
@@ -201,8 +201,18 @@
 namespace details {
 static bool isHostHal(const std::string& fqName);
 static sp<IServiceManager1_2> hostServiceManager();
+static sp<IServiceManager1_2> localServiceManager();
 }  // namespace details
 
+// apklayer patch (libhidl 0004): an instance with host HALs also runs its own hwservicemanager (docs/HIDL.md, Halium), which is not libhwbinder's
+// default (no /system/bin/hwservicemanager, so the host-forwarding stand-in below stays); what is not the host's goes to it. Not run
+// (no marker file), or not answering: the stand-in's old answers.
+#define APKLAYER_LOCAL(CALL, ...)                                         \
+    if (sp<IServiceManager1_2> local = details::localServiceManager()) {  \
+        auto r = local->CALL(__VA_ARGS__);                                \
+        if (r.isOk()) return r;                                           \
+    }
